= Writing and Editing

The heart of InkyCap: writing notes, formatting them, linking them, and make them easily findable with properties.

InkyCap keeps your text as plain Typst; the visual editor is a convenient layer over the Typst source not a separate format. These pages cover how to write comfortably and how to build the web of links that makes a notebox more than a folder of files.

== In this section

- #wikilink("2 - Editing Notes"). How notes open and how you work in them: the Source, Visual, and Reading modes, reading formats, the slash command, and the right sidebar.
- #wikilink("3 - Formatting Your Writing"). How to format text in InkyCap using Typst-native syntax, the slash menu, and the selection toolbar: bold, headings, lists, math, callouts, tables, images, and verse.
- #wikilink("3.1 - Formatting Examples"). A quick, example-driven cheat sheet: every common element shown as the markup you type beside the result you get.
- #wikilink("3.2 - Advanced Formatting"). The slash menu's Style category — page size, margins, columns, fonts, and spacing — and how these document-level set rules show in exports while appearing as a pill in the visual editor.
- #wikilink("4 - Links and Backlinks"). How to connect notes with wikilinks, link to headings, use aliases, follow external links, and read the automatic backlinks every note collects.
- #wikilink("5 - Tags"). How tags work in InkyCap: adding inline and property tags, browsing them in the flat tag sidebar, and using them to build Collections.
- #wikilink("6 - Note Properties"). How to use note properties, the friendly typed metadata panel that powers collections, search, and portable Typst querying.
- #wikilink("7 - Citations and Bibliography"). How to connect a bibliography (file or Zotero), browse and insert citations, choose a citation style, render the reference list, copy a formatted bibliography, and read citation warnings.

#callout("note")[ New to the editor? Begin with #wikilink("2 - Editing Notes"), then #wikilink("3 - Formatting Your Writing"). #wikilink("4 - Links and Backlinks") is the one concept that most changes how you work. ]
